Sandbox & Classroom Cost Optimization Proposal Script
Introduction
"Today I want to walk through a proposal for optimizing costs in our AWS Sandbox and Classroom environments.
While reviewing the Sandbox account costs, I found that a significant portion of the spend is coming from resources that appear to be underutilized or no longer actively used. The largest contributors were RDS and networking-related services, and some databases showed little to no CPU utilization.
The goal of this proposal is to introduce lifecycle management so that resources are automatically tracked, monitored, and eventually cleaned up when they are no longer needed." [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]

Current Challenges
"Today, when resources are created in Sandbox or Classroom environments, there is no automated way to determine who owns them, when they should expire, or whether they are still needed.
As a result, resources can remain deployed for months after testing is complete, which increases monthly cloud spend and makes ongoing management more difficult." [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]

Proposed Solution
"The proposal introduces an Automated Lifecycle Management process.
The idea is simple: every resource created in Sandbox or Classroom environments becomes part of a lifecycle process from day one.
Instead of manually tracking resources, we'll use automated tagging and lifecycle controls to manage them throughout their lifespan." [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]

Phase 1 – Step 1
"The first step is automated tagging.
Whenever a resource is created, it will automatically receive lifecycle-related tags such as:

ExpiresOn
LifecycleState
OwnerEmail

These tags provide ownership information and define how resources will be managed going forward." [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]

Phase 1 – Step 2
"The second step is onboarding existing resources.
We'll scan the current environment, apply lifecycle tags where needed, and identify resources that may be idle or no longer required.
At this stage we're primarily establishing visibility and governance. The onboarding process itself doesn't automatically delete resources." [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]

Risk Mitigation
"I want to emphasize that the proposal takes a cautious approach.
Before any destructive action is taken, backups or snapshots are performed.
The onboarding process only applies tags and improves visibility. Any future deletion activities would go through defined lifecycle processes and review steps." [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]

Future Phases
"Once the tagging and onboarding foundation is in place, future phases will introduce additional automation.
These phases include:

Lifecycle monitoring
Expiration notifications
Resource quarantine
Automated cleanup after review
Scheduled stop/start operations
Expansion into Classroom environments after successful Sandbox validation" [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]


Expected Benefits
"The primary benefit is cost reduction.
The analysis identified several opportunities to eliminate unnecessary spend through resource cleanup, rightsizing, and lifecycle controls.
In addition to cost savings, we'll gain better ownership tracking, improved governance, and a repeatable process that prevents future resource accumulation." [Sandbox_an...n_Proposal | Word]

Recommendation / Closing
"My recommendation is to start with Phase 1 by implementing automated tagging and onboarding existing resources into the lifecycle management system.
This gives us immediate visibility and establishes the foundation for future automation while minimizing operational risk.
Once we've validated the results in Sandbox, we can evaluate expanding the same approach to Classroom environments."
